 ## Overview
-Closed-Loop DPCM Decompression reconstructs volumetric trace data from compressed payloads using differential pulse-code modulation with a feedback loop to prevent error accumulation [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from open-loop variants by using the decoder's own reconstructed values as predictors for subsequent samples, ensuring bit-exact reconstruction in lossless contexts and stable error bounds in lossy contexts [S1, unofficial]. It is the primary decompression method for modern `.sdc` file formats, falling back to legacy decoding for backward compatibility [S1, unofficial].
+Closed-loop DPCM decompression reconstructs seismic trace data from compressed payloads by using a feedback loop where the decoder's reconstructed values serve as the prediction for subsequent samples [S1, unofficial]. Unlike open-loop methods that rely on stored deltas or raw quantized values without internal state feedback, this method ensures that quantization errors do not accumulate unbounded by correcting predictions against the actual quantized residuals [S1, unofficial]. It is primarily used in the SeismicDataCompressor project for decoding `.sdc` v3+ files, distinguishing itself from legacy decompression modes by supporting variable bit-depth storage and robust error handling [S1, unofficial].
 
